  narrativeContent: `For three hundred years Europeans looked for a sea route across the top of North America, and for most of that time the people who lived there watched them do it. The Inuit knew the coasts, the ice and the seasons; the searchers had ships, instruments and orders. The story of the Northwest Passage is the story of how those two kinds of knowledge met, usually badly, sometimes well, and of what the meeting cost.

## From the Atlantic

Martin Frobisher came in 1576 looking for a strait to China and found a bay, a mine of fool's gold, and the Inuit of Baffin Island, three of whom he carried to England to die. John Davis mapped the strait that bears his name in three voyages and treated the Inuit he met with curiosity rather than guns. Henry Hudson sailed through Hudson Strait in 1610 and was set adrift by his crew in the bay; Button, Bylot, Baffin, Munk, Foxe and James followed, and by 1632 it was clear that Hudson Bay had no exit to the west. Baffin had sailed round the bay that bears his name in 1616 and seen the sounds that led west, but he judged them closed, and no one went back for two hundred years. Middleton's voyage of 1742 and the quarrel with Dobbs that followed show how hard it was to give the idea up.

## From the Pacific

James Cook came through Bering Strait in 1778 and was stopped by ice at Icy Cape. The Russian Kotzebue found his sound in 1816. From then on the Pacific was the second door: Beechey waited at Kotzebue Sound in 1826 for Franklin, who never came, and the Franklin search ships Herald, Plover, Enterprise and Investigator all came that way in 1848-54. The coast between the two oceans was walked and rowed by Franklin in 1821 and 1826, by Richardson, by Dease and Thomas Simpson of the Hudson's Bay Company in 1837-39, with the Inuk Ouligbuck interpreting for all of them, until by 1839 the whole continental shore from Bering Strait to Boothia was on the map.

## The Navy's Arctic

After 1818 the Royal Navy, with nothing to do after Napoleon, made the passage its own. John Ross turned back at imaginary mountains in Lancaster Sound; Parry sailed through them the next year to Melville Island and wintered there, the first ships to winter in the archipelago, and found the ice beyond impassable. Ross came back privately in 1829 and spent four winters on Boothia, where a Netsilik band kept his crew alive and whose maps took his nephew James Clark Ross to the North Magnetic Pole. By 1845 the gaps on the chart were small, and Franklin was sent with two ships, 129 men and three years' food to close them.

## The search

Franklin wintered at Beechey Island, went down Peel Sound and was frozen in Victoria Strait in September 1846. He died in June 1847; the ships were abandoned in April 1848 and the 105 survivors died walking south. Nobody knew any of this for eleven years. Between 1848 and 1859 some forty expeditions looked for him, from both oceans: James Clark Ross at Port Leopold, the great squadrons of 1850-54 at Beechey Island and in Wellington Channel, McClure and Collinson from the Pacific, Lady Franklin's own ships under Kennedy, Bellot and McClintock, Kane in Smith Sound, Rae overland for the Hudson's Bay Company. They did not find Franklin alive, but they mapped the archipelago, proved the passage existed, and learned from the Inuit how to travel in it. The Inuit also knew the answer: In-nook-poo-zhe-jook told it to Rae in 1854, through William Ouligbuck, and England did not want to hear it. McClintock's Fox found the written record in 1859. Hall, with Tookoolito and Ebierbing, gathered the Inuit testimony in the 1860s; the wrecks of 2014 and 2016 lay where the Inuit had said.

## The passage sailed

Roald Amundsen read all of this before he sailed in 1903 in a small herring boat with six men. He followed Franklin's route, wintered two years at Gjoa Haven learning from the Netsilik, crept along the shallow mainland coast and met a whaler from San Francisco in August 1905. By then the question had changed from where the passage was to whose it was. Bernier's plaque at Parry's Rock in 1909 claimed the archipelago for Canada on the strength of the British searches; Sverdrup's Norwegian maps were bought out in 1930; and in 1999 the territory of Nunavut gave the land back a name in the language of the people who had been there throughout.`,
